          "Engage the Hispanic/Latino plurality (40.47%) with specific fluency on workforce and wage concerns: service-sector and hospitality workers, agricultural and construction workers, and immigrant households require representation on job training, wage floors, workplace rights, and healthcare access — not generic minority outreach.",
          "Address low college attainment (18.6%) through workforce development strategy: secondary education completion, apprenticeships, skills-based training, and career pathway access are the primary economic mobility mechanisms for most of the district's households and should anchor the incumbent's educational and economic development record.",
          "Treat unopposed status as a caution signal rather than evidence of electoral durability: the next competitive cycle will surface any gap between incumbent service to economic security concerns and constituent expectations, so active constituent engagement on housing, jobs, and wage growth now is the best defense against future vulnerability."
