          { name: "Renter Economic Security in an Affluent District", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 21.8% renter rate represents approximately 7,000-8,000 households whose economic exposure is fundamentally different from the homeowner majority: they face full vulnerability to Nevada's rising rental markets without the equity accumulation and property-tax stability that ownership provides. A challenger who builds a disciplined message around affordable housing, tenant protections, and the cost-of-living gap between renters and owners in this affluent district can organize this constituency into a swing bloc capable of narrowing a 63-37 margin significantly. The incumbent must engage renters with specific policy attention rather than treating them as a secondary constituency." },
          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Community Engagement & Economic Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "At 20.57%, the Hispanic/Latino community is the second-largest demographic group in NV-HD-31, yet affluent districts frequently underinvest in organizing and constituent service for this population. Economic mobility, education access, language-accessible services, and workforce development are material concerns for Hispanic residents in this district, and a challenger who organizes this community with authentic policy commitment rather than token acknowledgment can mobilize meaningful swing votes. The incumbent should treat this community as an active engagement priority, not an assumed-loyal segment of the base." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Structural Floor Rather Than an Active Consolidation Challenge", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 margin in Nevada is robust, but it is not a firewall against organized opposition. The renter constituency and Hispanic community represent plausible swing blocs, and a challenger who combines strong renter outreach with targeted Hispanic organizing while positioning the incumbent as taking these communities for granted can credibly narrow the margin. Any incumbent who treats 63% as a comfortable holding pattern rather than as the result of active homeowner consolidation and deliberate renter/Hispanic engagement is creating the opening for exactly this challenge." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Loyalty Based on Race, Income, or Homeownership Status", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, college-educated, white-majority districts often show partisan volatility when representatives assume constituent loyalty rather than engaging actively on material concerns. NV-HD-31's 67.67% white demographic does not automatically deliver 63% Republicans if renters feel neglected, if Hispanic residents experience the district's services as not designed for them, or if homeowners perceive governance failures on school funding or neighborhood investment. The current result reflects alignment, not inherited loyalty — and that alignment requires active maintenance through constituent service and delivered policy wins." }
