          "The strategic vulnerability in NV-SD-15 lies in the tension between its income level ($83,585) and its compositional volatility. A district at this income and education level in 2024 delivered a 55-45 Democratic result, but these voters—college-educated, suburban, professional-class—are precisely the constituencies that have proven susceptible to economic messaging, inflation concerns, and cost-of-living arguments. The 41.1% renter rate represents nearly 40% of households without property wealth as a stabilizing asset, which means housing costs, rental markets, and economic security concerns hit directly for a large segment of the constituency. The 26.45% Hispanic population is both a demographic growth story and an opportunity for the challenger: Hispanic voters in suburban Nevada districts have shown responsiveness to kitchen-table economics and cultural messaging, and in a 55-45 race, modest shifts in Hispanic turnout or candidate preference are sufficient to flip the seat. Any Democratic incumbent holding this seat must consolidate college-educated suburban voters on their actual economic anxiety (housing costs, education affordability), actively expand Hispanic voter engagement beyond demographic assumptions, and offer concrete answers to the 45% who chose the Republican candidate—particularly younger renters and working-class Hispanic households evaluating on cost of living and whether government is delivering for them."
