          "The strategic vulnerability in NV-SD-20 is not partisan but competence-based. The high median age (49.9 years), $88K+ income level, and college attainment mean this constituency evaluates candidates on whether they credibly address the material concerns of affluent, aging professionals: Medicare sustainability, prescription drug costs, and whether state government is administering these programs effectively without waste. The 38% Democratic share, while modest, represents college-educated professionals who may be persuadable on specific governance issues if they conclude the incumbent is not competent on healthcare access or retirement security. Any challenger to Stone must identify specific, documented governance failures — not rhetorical critiques but concrete examples of mismanagement or inadequate advocacy on retirement security or healthcare policy. Conversely, Stone's path to consolidating this seat is to lead with concrete accomplishments on healthcare access, Medicare advocacy, and senior constituent service that demonstrate fluency with the material concerns of a 49.9-year-old, college-educated, affluent constituency. This is a district where partisan margin is secure but where individual candidate competence on senior-focused governance is the differentiator between a held seat and a vulnerable one.",
